<script>
import LogCallDialog from 'crm/src/contacts/lib/LogCallDialog.abide'
import logCallDialogSource from 'crm/src/contacts/lib/LogCallDialog.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import logCallDialogTestSource from 'crm/src/contacts/lib/LogCallDialog.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import DeleteDeal from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DeleteDeal.abide'
import deleteDealSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DeleteDeal.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import deleteDealTestSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DeleteDeal.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import RenameDeal from 'crm/src/deals/lib/RenameDeal.abide'
import renameDealSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/RenameDeal.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import renameDealTestSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/RenameDeal.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A form over the page in a native modal">
        <Example
            id="dialog-log-call"
            title={'`<Dialog bind:open>` — a native modal you style'}
            summary="A call with Ada is logged from a form over the contact page. Log call keeps the note; Cancel, Escape or a click beside the form drops it."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/LogCallDialog.abide': logCallDialogSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/LogCallDialog.test.ts': logCallDialogTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <LogCallDialog />
        </Example>

        <p><code>Dialog</code> renders a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> and opens it with
            <code>showModal</code>. The browser puts it in the top layer, makes the page behind it
            inert, keeps focus inside it and closes it on Escape, so none of that is code in the
            component. Bind <code>open</code> to a value of yours: setting it opens the dialog, and
            every way it closes writes <code>false</code> back.</p>

        <p>A <code>&lt;form method="dialog"&gt;</code> inside closes it on submit, and
            <code>onclose</code> is handed the <code>value</code> of the button that submitted it.
            That is how the card tells Log call from Cancel without a handler on either button.
            Escape and a click on the backdrop close it with <code>''</code>. Set
            <code>dismissible={'{false}'}</code> to keep it open until the form submits or
            <code>open</code> turns false. The element carrying <code>autofocus</code> takes focus
            as it opens.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`prompts.confirm` before a change you cannot take back">
        <Example
            id="confirm-delete-deal"
            title={'`await prompts.confirm({ title, danger })` — a question that settles true or false'}
            summary="Deleting a deal takes its notes and its stage history with it, so the CRM asks first, and only Delete goes ahead."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/DeleteDeal.abide': deleteDealSource,
                'src/deals/lib/DeleteDeal.test.ts': deleteDealTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DeleteDeal />
        </Example>

        <p><code>prompts.confirm</code> queues a question and settles <code>true</code> only
            when the confirming button closed it. Cancel, Escape and the backdrop settle
            <code>false</code>, so <code>if (await prompts.confirm(…))</code> is the whole of the
            branch. <code>prompts.alert</code> takes the same options and settles once it closes, and
            <code>prompts.prompt</code> asks for text, below.
            A string in place of the options is the body. Both live on the <code>prompts</code>
            namespace, so none shadows the browser's own blocking <code>confirm</code>,
            <code>alert</code> and <code>prompt</code>, and a call missing its import fails to compile.</p>

        <p>Both show in <code>Prompts</code>, a <code>Dialog</code> you render once. The card
            renders it beside the list. An app renders it once, in its root layout, and every
            prompt on every page shows there, one at a time, in the order they were asked. Render
            it twice and both answer the same queue. Its role is <code>alertdialog</code>, and its
            cancel button takes focus, so Enter on a question declines it.</p>

        <p>A server has nobody to ask, so both throw there rather than wait forever. Call them
            from an event handler. The queue is a reactive value without <code>global</code>, so on
            a server each request has its own, and one visitor's prompt never renders into another
            visitor's page.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`prompts.prompt` for a line of text">
        <Example
            id="prompt-rename-deal"
            title={'`await prompts.prompt({ title, value })` — a line of text, or `undefined`'}
            summary="Renaming a deal asks for the new name in a dialog that starts from the old one. Rename keeps what was typed; Cancel keeps the old name."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/RenameDeal.abide': renameDealSource,
                'src/deals/lib/RenameDeal.test.ts': renameDealTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <RenameDeal />
        </Example>

        <p><code>prompts.prompt</code> shows an input in the same <code>Prompts</code> and settles
            with the text in it when the confirming button closed it, Enter included. Cancel,
            Escape and the backdrop settle <code>undefined</code>, so an empty string still means
            the person cleared the field and confirmed. The input starts from <code>value</code>,
            and takes focus as it opens.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Prompt options: `title`, `body`, `confirm`, `cancel`, `danger`, `mustType`, `value`, `placeholder`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>title</code></td><td>the heading, and the dialog's accessible name</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>body</code></td><td>the question or the message under it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>confirm</code></td><td>the confirming button's text, <code>'OK'</code> by default</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cancel</code></td><td>the declining button's text, <code>'Cancel'</code> by default; an alert has none</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>danger</code></td><td>writes <code>data-danger</code> on the dialog, for a warning's colors</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>mustType</code></td><td>a word the person types before the confirming button enables, matched ignoring case</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>value</code></td><td>the text a <code>prompts.prompt</code> input starts from, <code>''</code> by default</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>placeholder</code></td><td>that input's placeholder</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Reach for <code>mustType</code> where a slip would lose a lot: the name of a company with
            forty deals under it, typed back before the delete goes through.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Styling a dialog by its parts and states">
        <p>abide ships no styles for it. Your <code>class</code> lands on the
            <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, and everything inside <code>Prompts</code> is reached by
            its <code>data-part</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What it is</th><th>Target it with</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code></td><td>the root of <code>Dialog</code> and of <code>Prompts</code></td><td><code>class</code> on the component</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>::backdrop</code></td><td>the veil over the page</td><td><code>backdrop:bg-paper/70</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-open</code></td><td>on the root while it is open</td><td><code>data-[open]:…</code>, or <code>[data-open]</code> in CSS</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-danger</code></td><td>on <code>Prompts</code> while the prompt showing has <code>danger</code></td><td><code>data-[danger]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="form"</code></td><td>the <code>&lt;form method="dialog"&gt;</code> inside <code>Prompts</code></td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=form]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="title"</code></td><td>its <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code></td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=title]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="body"</code></td><td>its <code>&lt;p&gt;</code></td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=body]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="field"</code></td><td>the <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> around the input of a <code>prompts.prompt</code> or a <code>mustType</code></td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=field]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="input"</code></td><td>that <code>&lt;input&gt;</code></td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=input]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="actions"</code></td><td>the <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> holding the buttons</td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=actions]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="confirm"</code></td><td>the confirming button, <code>disabled</code> until <code>mustType</code> matches</td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=confirm]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="cancel"</code></td><td>the declining button</td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=cancel]]:…</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <Snippet source={deleteDealSource} path="src/deals/lib/DeleteDeal.abide" from={['<Prompts']} />

        <p>A state combines with a part: <code>data-[danger]:[&amp;_[data-part=confirm]]:bg-bad</code>
            turns the Delete button red only on a dangerous question. Written in a stylesheet, the
            same rule is <code>[data-danger] [data-part=confirm]</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
